      "Bash(git config --get:*)",
      "Bash(git config --list)",
      "Bash(git stash list:*)",
      "Bash(git fetch:*)",
      "Bash(git tag)",
      "Bash(git tag -l:*)",
      "Bash(git stash show:*)",
      {{/* The stash protocol the harness prescribes for a shared stash stack: push under a
           unique tag, apply by the entry's SHA. Neither is in the ask tier, but neither was
           allowed either, so a headless session was refused both (#861). A bare `apply`
           still meets claude_guard's bare-stash deny. */}}
      "Bash(git stash push -u -m:*)",
      "Bash(git stash apply:*)",
      "Bash(git worktree list:*)",
      "Bash(git reflog:*)",
      {{/* Reads the 2026-10-20 transcript replay found prompting. check-ignore only
           prints. `merge-tree --write-tree` writes the merged tree's objects
           into the object store and moves no ref, so nothing a reader sees changes; the
           older trivial-merge form is left out because nobody used it. `git grep` is NOT
           here: `-O<cmd>` runs a command, so a prefix rule would approve that too.
           claude_guard.judge allows it through remote_guards.git_readonly instead, which
           refuses `-O`. `git -C <path> <sub>` is judged there as `git <sub>` too. */}}
      "Bash(git check-ignore:*)",
      "Bash(git merge-tree --write-tree:*)",
      {{/* Branch-level git the reflog recovers. autoMode.allow already calls this routine
           in prose, which the classifier honours — but claude_guard.judge reads Bash()
           prefixes, not prose, so every `cd X && git switch Y` chain prompted anyway (56 of
           the 611 prompts on daniel-box in the week to 2026-08-21). `git checkout` as a bare
           prefix is deliberately absent: `git checkout -- <path>` discards working-tree
           changes and no reflog entry brings them back. Branch creation and switching carry
