           `.env.*`.

           Claude Code compiles each Read() deny twice: with the `ignore` package for the Read
           tool, and with its own glob-to-regex for the sandbox's read deny (2.1.282). Neither
           can say "all but these three". The sandbox compiler escapes `^`, so `[^e]` does not
           negate there, and `[!e]` is a negation only in `ignore`. So the rules spell out the
           complement with positive classes over printable ASCII. Both layers match
           case-insensitively, so each letter is `[eE]` and each class excludes both cases.
           Seatbelt also misreads a range that crosses from upper to lower case: measured with
           sandbox-exec, `[F-d]` and `[Z-a]` both match `e`. So each class is built from five
           bands that never cross a case boundary, ` -@`, `A-Z`, `[-backtick`, `a-z` and `{-~`,
           each minus the excluded letters.

           An `allowRead` exception cannot replace this. The sandbox keeps one only when its
           literal prefix sits under the session's cwd, so none reaches a worktree session,
           where 143 of about 150 template reads failed.

           The rules deny:
           - `.env.` itself, and any suffix whose first character is not e, s or t;
           - for each template word, every proper prefix (`.env.exa`), and every suffix that
